      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2011 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "A low level of high-density lipoprotein (HDL) is an acknowledged risk factor for coronary heart disease (CHD). HDL cholesterol (HDL-C) exerts its primary cardioprotective effect through a reverse cholesterol transport process, and suppression of this process has been the focus of the development of novel therapeutic agents for increasing HDL-C levels. Several strategies can be used to increase HDL-C levels to target cardiovascular risk reduction. This article presents a review of the biologic actions of HDL that can serve as a potential basis for antiatherosclerotic activity and discusses strategies for targeting HDL for CHD risk reduction."
      },
